{
  "id": "proj-flagstone-garden-path",
  "slug": "flagstone-garden-path",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Flagstone Garden Path",
  "summary": "Dry-laid flagstone on a compacted base with polymeric sand joints. No mortar to crack.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 12,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Call 811 to have buried utilities marked before you dig. Lay out a 3 x 20 ft path (60 sq ft) with stakes and mason's line. The quantities below assume that size.",
    "Dig the path out 6 1/2 in below the finished surface: 4 in for the base, 1 in for sand and about 1 1/2 in for the stone. Slope the bottom 1/4 in per foot to one side so water drains off. In hard-freeze areas dig 2 in deeper and add more base.",
    "Tamp the soil, then lay landscape fabric, overlapping the seams by 6 in.",
    "Spread the paver base in 2 in layers. Dampen each layer and tamp it hard before adding the next. Check it against the string line so the base is flat and keeps the drainage slope.",
    "Spread 1 in of leveling sand and screed it flat with a straight board.",
    "Dry-fit the flagstones like a puzzle with joints 1/2 in to 1 1/2 in wide. Trim high spots and corners with the brick set and hammer, wearing safety glasses.",
    "Bed each stone with the rubber mallet. Check it with the level against its neighbours, and add or scrape sand under it until it sits solid without rocking.",
    "Wait for a dry day with dry stones. Sweep polymeric sand into the joints, tamp it down, and top it up to 1/4 in below the stone surface. Sweep then blow every grain off the stone faces, or it will leave a haze.",
    "Mist the joints with a hose as the bag directs, without flooding them, and keep foot traffic off for 24 hours. Check that the product is rated for flagstone joints this wide before you buy."
  ],
